8	
9	/**
10	 * Shared handling for the owner {@code telephone}. Numbers are stored in E.164 form:
11	 * a leading {@code '+'} and country code are kept when present; otherwise country code
12	 * {@code '+61'} is assumed and a single leading {@code '0'} is dropped from the national
13	 * digits. Spaces, dashes and brackets are stripped, and the result must have 8 to 15
14	 * digits after the {@code '+'}.
15	 *
16	 * <p>Beyond that general range, the national number (the digits after the country code)
17	 * must have the exact length that country requires: {@code '+61'} takes 9 national
18	 * digits and {@code '+1'} takes 10. Other country codes are only bound by the general
19	 * 8-to-15 range.
20	 *
21	 * <p>So {@code '0412 345 678'} is stored as {@code '+61412345678'}.
22	 */
23	public final class Telephones {
24	
25	    // Formatting characters allowed inside a supplied number: spaces, dashes and brackets.
26	    private static final Pattern SEPARATORS = Pattern.compile("[\\s\\-()]");
27	
28	    // E.164: a '+' followed by 8 to 15 digits.
